From 60f70e26dd1e4507a34c1849d61266b945f8d2fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 10:40:31 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 07/17] rdna-boosts: block 07: k-quant boosts (mmvq VDR, Q6_K
 MMQ, Q1_0, grid pad)

- old-10: per-type mmvq VDR/nwarps for Q4_K/Q5_K/Q6_K/Q8_0, dense Q6_K VDR=2
  and the MoE-expert VDR split.
- old-04 MMQ: GGML_CUDA_MMQ_J_MAX and the mmq-vec-dot x_scale/x_df hoist.
- old-15: the Q1_0 ggml_cuda_byte_perm port (vecdotq.cuh + mmq-load-tiles.cuh),
  the gfx1201 grid-stall pad (common.cuh + mmvf.cu), the mmvf multi-token
  cross-warp reduction and the mmq-vec-dot #pragma unroll 1 for gfx12.
- old-04's MUL_MAT Q6_K/Q8_0 test shapes.
- r169: dense Q6_K keeps upstream #30517's VDR=2 on every arch (measured on
  gfx1151: within noise of VDR=1 at B=1/B=4 and d16384, +1.2 % at batched B=8,
  width-pure against draft-mtp).  -DGGML_HIP_Q6K_DENSE_VDR1 restores the
  pre-#30517 VDR=1 association order for a consumer that gates on output
  identity; the MoE-expert VDR is per kernel and unaffected.

NOTE: the mmvq multi-row/row-loop decode hunks (old-15 PR #57) are NOT here --
they are interleaved with the fused-MoE item-split in the same function and are
deferred to 09 (see PHASE1-STATUS.md).

Re-based onto a691b2345 (mmvq.cu conflicted, 2 hunks: the per-architecture MMVQ table
id).  The block splits the old combined mapping -- RDNA3_5 (gfx1151) gets its own table
(nwarps swept), real RDNA2 (gfx103x) gets the Navi21 table -- where upstream maps all of
RDNA2 || RDNA3_5 to the RDNA2 table.  The delivery's split is kept and upstream's new
RDNA4_M family is folded into it (RDNA4_M -> the RDNA2 table) on both the device and the
host table-id paths.  Build green; same-seed greedy text byte-identical with MMVQ on/off
(33 chars, sha 3de206c8878b) on Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B.

r290 amendment -- the VERIFY-band WMMA iu8 matvec family (contributor burnoutIA, PR #317; folded here
because this block is the delivery's decode-numerics block: it owns vecdotq.cuh's vec_dot_* family and
the mmvq K-split loop, and its contract is already "same real-number result, different addition order").

  * mmvq-wmma.cu/.cuh (new) + a fall-through in ggml_cuda_mul_mat_vec_q: for a dense, unfused MUL_MAT at
    N..8 columns on RDNA4, compute the product with wmma_i32_16x16x16_iu8, dequantizing each weight ONCE
    for all the verified columns.  Q4_K, Q5_K, Q6_K and IQ4_XS in their GGUF layout on the existing q8_1
    activations, plus Q8_0 (r290, ours: no dequantization, no min term, no table -- and its own per-format
    stride unit, because a Q8_0 block is 32 elements where the K-quants' is 256).  Q5_K's min term is
    d.SUM(q) through a ones matrix, as vec_dot_q5_K_q8_1 does (ds.y moves the outputs ~1 % and drops the
    DFlash2 acceptance).  Numerics against the K-split kernel: ~3.2e-7 relative (max 4.2e-5 on a 130 scale)
    at widths 2..8 for every covered type, i.e. rounding order only.
  * --wmma-verify off|auto|N (common), alias GGML_CUDA_WMMA_MMVQ=N, applied per device through the backend
    registry's proc-address table (no new cross-backend interface member in this block).
  * OPT-IN and WIDTH-SPLIT BY DESIGN: 1 column always keeps the K-split kernel, so while the option is ON
    the byte-identical greedy/verify contract (GREEDY-PURITY.md invariant 1) does NOT hold.  Default is
    OFF, and with it off nothing on the default path is reached -- this block's own contract is unchanged.
  * Measured (r289, 27B UD-Q4_K_M and Q8_0, 1 and 2 GPUs, finite work): width 8 +9.0 % (1 GPU) / +10.6 %
    (2 GPU tensor split) for the K-quants and +4.2 % for Q8_0; width 7 +2..7.1 %; width 6 -4.4..+1.9 %
    (split-dependent); widths 2..5 -0.5..-5.7 %.  Hence `auto` = 7, the first width it wins.  End to end
    (DFlash2 n=7, recall): cycle 37.0 -> 33.5 ms on the 2-GPU Q4_K_M split (+10.4 % tokens/s at identical
    acceptance), 43.6 -> 41.8 ms on Q8_0 (+4.2 %).  Not where the width is >8: those rounds are MMQ, so
    adaptive MTP n12 is neutral.  Gate: coherence (off == the r289 build, byte for byte) plus the four-axis
    A/B and llama-batched-bench with it on; ENVIRONMENT.md, GREEDY-PURITY.md and patches/README.md carry
    the numbers.

Co-authored-by: burnoutIA <72673971+burnoutIA@users.noreply.github.com>
